      <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-surface-900 sm:text-4xl">TikTok comment moderation: how to moderate TikTok comments as a brand or a media outlet</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-surface max-w-none text-surface-600">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">
        On TikTok, a video can go from a few hundred views to several million overnight, and the comments
        follow. For a brand or a newsroom, that means insults, spam, scams and off-topic fights can pile up
        under your logo while the team is asleep. This guide covers TikTok's built-in comment moderation
        tools, where they stop, and how to organise the work so it holds up when a video takes off.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">The short answer</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>TikTok gives you real tools:</strong> a spam and offensive comment filter, filtered keywords, manual approval of all comments, who-can-comment settings, bulk actions and LIVE moderators.</li>
        <li><strong>They match words, not meaning.</strong> Misspellings, emojis, slang and sarcasm get through, and fair comments can be caught.</li>
        <li><strong>At volume, the work is human:</strong> write your rules, prepare response templates, define who escalates what, and have a crisis plan for viral videos.</li>
        <li><strong>OpenModeration does not connect to TikTok.</strong> It moderates Facebook, Instagram, YouTube and websites from one console; if you also need TikTok, <a href={BOOKING_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">book a call</a> to discuss your case.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">What comment moderation tools does TikTok offer?</h2>
      <p>
        TikTok's comment settings sit in the app's privacy settings and on each video. Menu labels change
        between app versions, but the following features have been available for several years.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Who can comment.</strong> You decide whether everyone, only friends or nobody can comment, for the whole account or for a single video. You can also switch comments off on one post.</li>
        <li><strong>Filter spam and offensive comments.</strong> TikTok hides comments its own systems consider spam or offensive. It is a quick win, but you cannot see the list or adjust the threshold.</li>
        <li><strong>Filter keywords.</strong> You add words or phrases. Comments containing them do not appear publicly and wait in your filtered comments until you approve or delete them.</li>
        <li><strong>Filter all comments.</strong> Every new comment is held for review. Only the ones you approve become visible.</li>
        <li><strong>Delete, report and act in bulk.</strong> From the comment section you can delete or report a comment, or select several comments or accounts and delete, report or block them in one go.</li>
        <li><strong>Pin a comment.</strong> You keep one comment at the top of the thread, for example a clarification or an answer to a recurring question.</li>
        <li><strong>Restrict and block accounts.</strong> Restricting limits what an account can do on your content without cutting it off completely; blocking stops it interacting with you.</li>
        <li><strong>LIVE moderators.</strong> During a LIVE, you can name trusted people who can mute viewers and remove comments, and you can set keyword filters specific to the stream.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-surface-200">
