    <div class="prose prose-surface mt-10 max-w-none text-surface-600">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">How should you configure TikTok's comment filters?</h2>
      <p>A sensible starting point for a brand or media account looks like this.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Turn on the spam and offensive comment filter.</strong> It costs nothing and removes the most obvious noise.</li>
        <li><strong>Build a keyword list in layers.</strong> Start with insults and slurs, then add scam patterns ("DM me to earn", fake giveaway phrases), competitor handles if you do not want them promoted, and the names of people you must protect, such as employees or minors.</li>
        <li><strong>Add the variants.</strong> Common misspellings, spaced-out letters and the emojis used as insults in your community. Review the list every month.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep "who can comment" open.</strong> Open comments are part of how TikTok works. Restrict them only on sensitive videos.</li>
        <li><strong>Use "filter all comments" selectively.</strong> On a video about a sensitive topic, a recall or a news story involving victims, manual approval is often the safest choice.</li>
        <li><strong>Check filtered comments every day.</strong> Keyword filters catch innocent comments too. Approving them quickly shows your audience that you are not silencing them.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Why TikTok's tools are not enough at volume</h2>
      <p>The built-in tools are designed for creators managing their own account. Brands and media run into the same limits sooner or later.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Words, not meaning.</strong> A keyword list cannot tell an insult from a quote, or a joke from a threat. "Great job as usual" under a product recall contains no banned word.</li>
        <li><strong>Constant workarounds.</strong> TikTok users are inventive: letters swapped for numbers, emojis, invented slang. A list is always one step behind.</li>
        <li><strong>Several languages.</strong> International accounts receive comments in many languages, and each list has to be rebuilt language by language.</li>
        <li><strong>Your own rules are not in the tool.</strong> TikTok's filter knows its Community Guidelines, not your policy on competitor links, political debate or customers posting order numbers in public.</li>
        <li><strong>No written reason and no history.</strong> When a comment is filtered or deleted, nothing records which rule applied or who decided. That makes internal reviews and complaints hard to handle.</li>
        <li><strong>Manual approval does not scale.</strong> Filtering all comments is safe, but on a video with thousands of comments, someone has to read every one of them.</li>
        <li><strong>One more inbox.</strong> TikTok has its own interface, separate from the tools you use for Facebook, Instagram or YouTube.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">What rules should a brand set for TikTok comments?</h2>
      <p>
        Before choosing settings, write down what you accept and what you remove. One page is enough, and
        the same rules should apply on every network. For each case, decide the action.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Leave online and answer:</strong> criticism, complaints, questions, disagreement, even when the tone is rough.</li>
        <li><strong>Filter or delete:</strong> insults and harassment, hate speech, threats, spam and scam links, impersonation of your brand, personal data (phone numbers, addresses, order numbers).</li>
        <li><strong>Escalate:</strong> threats against people, possible illegal content, comments involving minors, legal claims, anything a journalist could quote.</li>
        <li><strong>Restrict or block the account:</strong> repeat offenders, bots and accounts created only to attack you.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Our <a href="/blog/comment-moderation-guide" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">comment moderation guide</a> explains
        how to write these rules, and the guide on <a href="/blog/handle-negative-comments-trolls" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">handling negative comments and trolls</a> covers
        the grey areas.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Which response templates work on TikTok?</h2>
      <p>
        TikTok replies are short and informal, and a reply to a comment can even be a video. Templates save
        time, but adapt each one so it does not sound automatic.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Customer complaint:</strong> "Sorry about that! Send us a DM with your order number and we'll sort it out today."</li>
        <li><strong>Recurring question:</strong> answer once, then pin the comment or reply with a short video.</li>
        <li><strong>Misinformation:</strong> "To clarify: [fact], here is our statement [where to find it]." Pin it if the question keeps coming back.</li>
        <li><strong>Heated debate between users:</strong> "Happy to see the discussion, please keep it respectful. Insults will be removed."</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Never ask for personal data in a public reply. Move the conversation to direct messages or to your customer service.</p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">How do you handle a crisis on a viral TikTok video?</h2>
      <p>Viral videos are where moderation fails most often. Prepare the playbook before you need it.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Detect.</strong> Set alerts on unusual comment volume and on mentions of your brand. Someone should be on call in the evening and at weekends during campaigns.</li>
        <li><strong>Tighten the settings on that video.</strong> Switch to filtering all comments, or limit who can comment, for a few hours. Add the words of the crisis to your keyword filter.</li>
        <li><strong>Pin a factual reply.</strong> One clear message, validated by communications, is better than dozens of individual replies.</li>
        <li><strong>Escalate.</strong> Threats, doxxing and possible illegal content go to the people who handle legal and security issues, with screenshots.</li>
        <li><strong>Review in batches.</strong> Approve the fair comments, including the critical ones, and delete what breaks your rules. Use bulk actions for spam waves.</li>
        <li><strong>Debrief.</strong> Update the keyword list and the templates with what you learned.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">How do you organise TikTok moderation alongside other networks?</h2>
      <p>
        Most brands and media are on TikTok, Instagram, Facebook and YouTube at the same time, often with a
        website on top. The risk is having four sets of rules and four people improvising.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One set of rules for every network,</strong> with notes where a platform needs a different action.</li>
        <li><strong>Clear roles:</strong> who moderates, who answers, who validates sensitive replies, who handles escalations.</li>
        <li><strong>Shared response templates</strong> adapted to each platform's tone.</li>
        <li><strong>A single log of sensitive decisions,</strong> even if it is a shared document for TikTok.</li>
        <li><strong>Automate where you can,</strong> so the team spends its time on the network that still needs hands-on work.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is where a moderation tool helps. OpenModeration moderates
        <a href="/integrations/facebook" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">Facebook Pages</a>,
        <a href="/integrations/instagram" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">Instagram professional accounts</a>,
        YouTube channels and websites from one console. It applies your rules and your use cases, hides or rejects
        comments, sends doubtful cases to human review, gives a written reason for every decision and keeps
        an audit trail. That frees up the time your team needs to moderate TikTok by hand. See also our
        <a href="/blog/social-media-moderation-guide" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">social media moderation guide</a>.
      </p>

